  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 219 PM CDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\nContinued dry and seasonable conditions are expected Thursday, \nas the upper ridge retrogrades west and builds across the \nsouthwest CONUS. At the same time, all ensemble and \ndeterministic solutions have a strong shortwave rounding the top\nof the ridge over the northern Plains. 850mb moisture transport\nahead of this wave will initially be pointed to our west, \nkeeping the local area dry during the daylight hours Thursday. \n\nThursday Night-Friday...as expected at this time range, the latest \ndeterministic model data has varying solutions of the evolution of \nthe aforementioned shortwave. Strong moisture transport evident by \nPWs increasing to 1.6-1.7\" range (90th percentile) and large scale \nforcing to support widespread showers and thunderstorms. A dprog/dt \nof 24-hr QPF greater than 0.50\" from the NBM has shown an increase \ninto the 70-75% range. This is good news and will be much \nwelcomed for the CWA, as most of the region has seen less than \n0.05\" in the past 7 days. Taking a look at the severe risk, the\nLREF joint probabilities of MUCAPE>500 J/Kg & deep layer \nshear>30kts and NCAR ML severe probs would continue to suggest a\nvery low risk for severe storms. Better support for organized \nconvection will be displaced further to the south over eastern \nMO. Regarding temperatures, latest NBM has come down for highs \non Friday into the upper 70s/low 80s. However, some further\nlowering into the mid 70s will be certainly possible with \nprecip/clouds through much of the day. \n\nSaturday-Early Next Week...lingering precip chances will be \nseen Saturday AM, exiting the area from northwest to southeast \nby mid-day. Following the passage of the shortwave, the upper \nlevel flow will become northerly as an area of high pressure \nsets up over the northern Great Lakes. This will result in a \nrather pleasant start to the month of August. Dry conditions \nand cooler temperatures are forecast, with highs in the upper \n70s to lower 80s.",
